    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Monroe City was named after its founder, Monroe Alton, in the early 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monroe_City%2C_Indiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1856 the town first called Nashville, then Lovelydale, before settling on Monroe City after a state law.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.usacitiesonline.com/incountymonroecity.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A 1908 plan aimed to bring a railroad through Monroe City, though it never materialized.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.usacitiesonline.com/incountymonroecity.htm"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Monroe City sits in Harrison Township within Knox County, Indiana in the southwestern part of the state.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monroe_City%2C_Indiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town is served by the South Knox School Corporation, the local public school district.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monroe_City%2C_Indiana"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Monroe City lies about 105 miles south of Indianapolis, the state capital.",
